== A Brief Account of the Author’s Imprisonment

318\. Having made profession of the glorious gospel of Christ a long time, and preached it for about five years, I was arrested at a meeting of good people in the country, among whom, had they let me alone, I would have preached that day. But they took me away from among them and brought me before a justice, who, after I had offered security for my appearing at the next sessions, still committed me to prison, because my sureties would not consent to be bound that I would preach no more to the people.

319\. At the sessions after that, I was indicted as an upholder and maintainer of unlawful assemblies and conventicles, and for not conforming to the national worship of the Church of England. And after some discussion #emph[there] with the justices, they took my plain dealing with them as a confession, as they called it, #emph[of the indictment, and sentenced me to perpetual banishment, because I refused to conform]. So, being delivered up again into the jailers’ hands, I was taken back to prison, and have now lain there a full twelve years, waiting to see what God will allow these men to do with me.

320\. In this condition I have continued with much contentment, through grace, but have met with many turnings and goings upon my heart, from the Lord, from Satan, and from my own corruptions. By all of them (glory be to Jesus Christ!) I have also received, among many things, much conviction, instruction, and understanding, of which I shall not discourse at large here; I will only give you, in a hint or two, a word that may stir up the godly to bless God and to pray for me, and also to take encouragement, should the case be their own, #emph[not] to fear what man can do to them.

321\. I never in all my life had so great an entrance into the Word of God as now. Those scriptures that I saw nothing in before are made in this place and state to shine upon me. Jesus Christ also was never more real and apparent than now; here I have seen Him and felt Him indeed. Oh, that word, “We have not preached to you#super[+] cunningly devised fables” (2 Pet 1:16), and that one, “God raised Christ from the dead and gave Him glory, so that your#super[+] faith and hope might be in God” (1 Pet 1:21), were blessed words to me in this my imprisoned condition.

322\. These three or four scriptures also have been a great refreshment to me in this condition (John 14:1–4; 16:33; Col 3:3, 4; Heb 12:22–24). So that at times, when I have been in the savor of them, I have been able to laugh at destruction, and to fear neither the horse nor his rider (Job 39:18). I have had sweet sights of the forgiveness of my sins in this place, and of my being with Jesus in another world. Oh, “Mount Zion, the heavenly Jerusalem, the myriads of angels, and God the Judge of all, and the spirits of the righteous made perfect, and Jesus” (Heb 12:22–24) have been sweet to me in this place. I have seen #emph[that] here which I am persuaded I shall never, while in this world, be able to express. I have seen a truth in that scripture, “Though you#super[+] have not seen Him, you love Him; and though you do not see Him now, you believe in Him and rejoice with an inexpressible and glorious joy” (1 Pet 1:8).

323\. I never knew what it was for God to stand by me at all turns, and at every attempt of Satan to afflict me, etc., as I have found Him since I came in here. For as fears have presented themselves, so have supports and encouragements. Yes, when I have been startled, as it were, at nothing but my own shadow, yet God, being very tender toward me, has not allowed me to be harassed, but would strengthen me against it all with one scripture or another; so much so that I have often said, “Were it lawful, I could pray for greater trouble, for the sake of the greater comfort” (Eccl 7:14; 2 Cor 1:5).

324\. Before I came to prison, I saw what was coming, and had two considerations especially warm upon my heart. The first was how to be able to endure, should my imprisonment be long and tedious; the second was how to be able to face death, should that be my portion here. For the first of these, that scripture (Col 1:11) was great instruction to me, namely, to pray to God “to be strengthened with all power according to His glorious might, for all patience and long-suffering with joy.” For no less than a whole year before I was imprisoned, I could seldom go to prayer without this sentence, or sweet petition, as it were thrusting itself into my mind and persuading me that if ever I was to go through long-suffering, I must have all patience, especially if I would endure it joyfully.

325\. As to the second consideration, that saying was of great use to me, “But we had the sentence of death in ourselves, in order that we would not trust in ourselves, but in God, who raises the dead” (2 Cor 1:9). By this scripture I was made to see that if ever I was to suffer rightly, I must first pass a sentence of death upon everything that can properly be called a thing of this life, even to reckon myself, my wife, my children, my health, my enjoyments, and all, as dead to me, and myself as dead to them.

326\. The second was to live upon God who is invisible, as Paul said in another place; the way not to faint is to fix our eyes “not on what is seen, but on what is unseen. For what is seen is temporary, but what is unseen is eternal” (2 Cor 4:18). And so I reasoned with myself: If I provide only for a prison, then the whip comes unexpectedly, and so does the pillory. Again, if I provide only for these, then I am not fit for banishment. Further, if I conclude that banishment is the worst, then if death comes, I am taken by surprise. So I see that the best way to go through sufferings is to trust in God through Christ as touching the world to come; and as touching this world, to count the grave my house, to make my bed in darkness, to say to corruption, “You are my father,” and to the worm, “You are my mother and sister”: that is, to make these things familiar to me.

327\. But in spite of these helps, I found myself a man, and beset with infirmities. The parting with my wife and poor children has often been to me in this place like the pulling of the flesh from my bones; and that not only because I am somewhat too fond of these great mercies, but also because I would often call to mind the many hardships, miseries, and wants that my poor family was likely to meet with should I be taken from them, #emph[especially my poor blind child], who lay nearer my heart than all I had besides. Oh, the thought of the hardship I thought my blind one might go under would break my heart to pieces.

328\. “Poor child,” I thought, “what sorrow are you likely to have for your portion in this world! You must be beaten, must beg, suffer hunger, cold, nakedness, and a thousand calamities, though I cannot now endure that the wind should blow upon you.” But yet, recalling myself, I thought, “I must venture you#super[+] all with God, though it cuts to the quick to leave you.” Oh, I saw that in this condition I was like a man who was pulling down his house upon the head of his wife and children; yet I thought, “I must do it, I must do it.” And now I thought of those two milk cows that were to carry the ark of God into another country and leave their calves behind them (1 Sam 6:10–12).

329\. But what helped me in this temptation was various considerations, of which I will name three in particular here. The first was the consideration of these two scriptures: “Leave your fatherless children; I will preserve their lives. Let your widows trust in Me.” And again, “The #smallcaps[Lord] said: ‘Surely it will go well with your remnant; surely I will cause the enemy to treat you well in the time of evil’” (Jer 49:11; 15:11).

330\. I had also this consideration: that if I should now venture all for God, I engaged God to take care of my concerns; but if I forsook Him and His ways for fear of any trouble that might come to me or mine, then I would not only prove my profession false, but would also count my concerns not so safe if left at God’s feet, while I stood to and for His name, as they would be if they were under my own care, though with the denial of the way of God. This was a smarting consideration, and was like spurs to my flesh. That scripture also greatly helped to fasten it more upon me, where Christ prays against Judas that God would disappoint him in all his selfish thoughts, which moved him to sell his master. Please read it soberly (Ps 109:6–20).

331\. I had also another consideration, and that was the dread of the torments of hell, which I was sure those must share in who for fear of the cross shrink from their profession of Christ, His words and laws, before the sons of men. I thought also of the glory that He had prepared for those who in faith, and love, and patience stood by His ways before them. These things, I say, have helped me when the thought of the misery that both I and mine might be exposed to for the sake of my profession has lain pinching on my mind.

332\. When I have indeed imagined that I might be banished for my profession, then I have thought of that scripture, “They were stoned, they were sawed in two, they were tempted, they were put to death by the sword. They went around in sheepskins and goatskins, destitute, oppressed, and mistreated (the world was not worthy of them)” (Heb 11:37, 38), though people thought them too bad to dwell and abide among them. I have also thought of that saying, “In town after town the Holy Spirit warns me that chains and afflictions await me” (Acts 20:23). I have truly thought that my soul and that word have at times reasoned together about the sore and sad state of a banished and exiled condition: how such men are exposed to hunger, to cold, to perils, to nakedness, to enemies, and a thousand calamities; and at last, it may be, to die in a ditch like a poor, forlorn, and desolate sheep. But I thank God that so far I have not been moved by these most #emph[delicate] reasonings, but have rather, by them, more approved my heart to God.

333\. I will tell you of a pretty business. I was once, above all the rest, in a very sad and low condition for many weeks; at which time also, being only a young prisoner and not acquainted with the laws, I had this lying heavily upon my spirit: that my imprisonment might end at the gallows, for all that I could tell. Now therefore Satan pressed hard upon me, to put me out of heart, by suggesting this to me: “But what if, when you come indeed to die, you should be in this condition, that is, not to savor the things of God, nor to have any evidence upon your soul for a better state hereafter?” For indeed at that time all the things of God were hidden from my soul.

334\. So when I first began to think of this, it was a great trouble to me; for I thought to myself that in the condition I was now in, I was not fit to die, nor indeed did I think I could, if I should be called to it. Besides, I thought to myself, if I should make a scrambling shift to clamber up the ladder, I would yet, either with quaking or other symptoms of fainting, give occasion to the enemy to reproach the way of God and His people for their timidity. This, therefore, lay with great trouble upon me, for it seemed to me I was ashamed to die with a pale face and tottering knees for such a cause as this.

335\. So I prayed to God that He would comfort me, and give me strength to do and suffer what He should call me to; yet no comfort appeared, but all continued hidden. I was also at this time so really possessed with the thought of death that I was often as if I were on a ladder with the rope about my neck. Only this was some encouragement to me: I thought I might now have an opportunity to speak my last words to a multitude, which I thought would come to see me die. “And,” I thought, “if it must be so, if God will convert even one soul by my very last words, I shall not count my life thrown away or lost.”

336\. But still all the things of God were kept out of my sight, and still the tempter followed me with, “But where must you go when you die? What will become of you? Where will you be found in another world? What evidence do you have for heaven and glory, and an inheritance among those who are sanctified?” So I was tossed for many weeks, and did not know what to do. At last this consideration fell with weight upon me, that it was for the word and way of God that I was in this condition; so I was bound not to flinch a hair’s breadth from it.

337\. I thought also that God might choose whether He would give me comfort now or at the hour of death; but I might not therefore choose whether I would hold to my profession or not. I was bound, but He was free. Yes, it was my duty to stand by His word, whether He would ever look upon me or save me at the last. So, I thought, the point being so, I am for going on and venturing my eternal state with Christ, whether I have comfort here or not. “If God does not come in,” I thought, “I will leap off the ladder even blindfold into eternity, sink or swim, come heaven, come hell. Lord Jesus, if You will catch me, do; if not, I will venture for Your name.”

338\. No sooner was I fixed upon this resolution than that word dropped upon me, “Does Job serve God for nothing?” As if the accuser had said, “Lord, Job is no upright man; he serves You for selfish ends. Have You not placed a hedge on every side around him? But stretch out Your hand and strike all that he has, and he will surely curse You to Your face.” “How now,” I thought, “is this the sign of an upright soul, to desire to serve God when all is taken from him? Is he a godly man who will serve God for nothing rather than give up? Blessed be God; then I hope I have an upright heart, for I am resolved, God giving me strength, never to deny my profession, though I have nothing at all for my pains.” And as I was considering this, that scripture was set before me (Ps 44:12–26).

339\. Now my heart was full of comfort, for I hoped it was sincere. I would not have been without this trial for much. I am comforted every time I think of it, and I hope I shall bless God forever for the teaching I have had by it. I might relate many more of the dealings of God with me, but “these, out of the spoils won in battle, I have dedicated to maintain the house of God” (1 Chr 26:27).
